HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ccoonnffiigg utility appeared in 4.1BSD.

     Before support for --xx was introduced, ooppttiioonnss IINNCCLLUUDDEE__CCOONNFFIIGG__FFIILLEE in‐
     cluded entire configuration file that used to be embedded in the new ker‐
     nel.  This meant that strings(1) could be used to extract it from a ker‐
     nel: to extract the configuration information, you had to use the com‐
     mand:

           strings -n 3 kernel | sed -n 's/^___//p'

